Here's a simple example of polymorphism using Java. The concept illustrated here is that various subclasses can implement methods that behave differently but can be called in the same way from an object of the parent class. 

```java
// This is the superclass with one method that will be overridden in the subclasses.
class Animal {
    // a method showing what sound the animal makes
    void makeSound(){
        System.out.println("The animal makes a sound");
    }
}

// This is a first subclass which extends from Animal superclass.
class Cat extends Animal {
    // overriding the makeSound() method
    @Override
    void makeSound() {
        System.out.println("The cat meows");
    }
}

// This is a second subclass which extends from Animal superclass.
class Dog extends Animal {
    // overriding the makeSound() method
    @Override
    void makeSound() {
        System.out.println("The dog barks");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args){
        Animal myAnimal = new Animal();  // Create a Animal object
        Animal myCat = new Cat();  // Create a Cat object
        Animal myDog = new Dog();  // Create a Dog object

        // Call the makeSound() method on the myAnimal, myDog and myCat
        // This demonstrates that the method can be called polymorphically.
        myAnimal.makeSound();
        myCat.makeSound();
        myDog.makeSound();
    }
}
```
In this code, `Animal` is the superclass and `Cat` and `Dog` are subclasses. Both subclasses override the `makeSound()` method from the superclass to make it specific for each subclass.

Then, in the main function, we create an object of each class (superclass and subclasses). When we call the `makeSound()` method for each object, the specific implementation for each subclass is used. This, despite the fact that the reference type for both subclass objects `myCat` and `myDog` is the superclass `Animal`.

This is polymorphism in action. The same method call results in different behaviors depending on the actual object the method is called on. Despite the "form" (i.e., reference type) being the same, the "behavior" is different, hence "poly-morphism".

