    public Counter() {
        count++;  // Increment count whenever a new object is created
    }
}
```

In this example, the "count" variable is declared as static. Every time an object of the Counter class is created, the count variable is incremented. As the count variable is shared among all objects, the value of count will be the same for all instances of the class.

2. Static Methods:
A static method belongs to the class and can be invoked without creating an object of the class. Static methods can access only static variables and other static methods.

Example:

```java
public class MathUtils {
    public static int add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }
}
```

In this example, the add() method is declared as static. It can be called using the class name, MathUtils.add(2, 3), without creating an instance of the MathUtils class.

3. Static Nested Classes:
A nested class declared as static is called a static nested class. It does not have access to non-static members of the outer class and can be instantiated without an instance of the outer class.

Example:

```java
public class OuterClass {
    static class InnerClass {
        public void print() {
            System.out.println("Static nested class");
        }
    }
}
```

In this example, the InnerClass is declared as static. It can be accessed using the OuterClass name, OuterClass.InnerClass inner = new OuterClass.InnerClass(), without creating an instance of the OuterClass.

Pros of using the "static" keyword:
1. Memory Efficiency: Static members are stored in a shared memory space, reducing memory consumption compared to non-static members that are duplicated for each object instance.
2. Global Accessibility: Static members can be accessed without creating an instance of the class, providing global accessibility throughout the program.
3. Code Readability: It makes it clear that the member belongs to the class itself and not to an instance, improving code readability.

Cons of using the "static" keyword:
1. Limited Flexibility: Static members cannot be overridden or hidden, which limits the flexibility of the code design.
2. Increased Coupling: Static members can introduce tight coupling between classes, as they are not encapsulated within object instances.
3. Thread Safety: Concurrent access to static members can lead to race conditions and requires additional synchronization mechanisms to ensure thread safety.

Performance Implications:
Using the "static" keyword generally does not have significant performance implications. However, accessing static members might be slightly faster compared to instance members, as they can be accessed directly from the class area without any indirection. Additionally, static methods can be invoked without the overhead of creating an object. However, the impact on performance is generally negligible and should not be a primary concern when deciding to use the "static" keyword.

